On darwin-ppc, bool is 4 bytes by default (legacy Apple ABI), which breaks
V8's pervasive assumption of 1-byte bools in two ways:

1. ThreadLocalTop's two bool members grow the struct from 96 to 100 bytes,
   violating ThreadLocalTop::kSizeInBytes and shifting every subsequent
   IsolateData field (including the builtin entry table) away from its
   static layout constant — this is what made IsolateData's
   AssertPredictableLayout fail on Darwin ppc.

2. Generated code reads C++ bool flags with 1-byte loads at offset 0. With
   a big-endian 4-byte bool the value byte is at offset 3, so such loads
   always read 0: Debug::is_active_, Debug::hook_on_function_call_,
   Isolate::promise_hook_or_async_event_delegate_,
   Isolate::promise_hook_or_debug_is_active_or_async_event_delegate_,
   and Isolate::is_profiling_.

Use uint8_t for these members, following the existing upstream idiom
(Heap::is_marking_flag_, IsolateData::stack_is_iterable_ are already
uint8_t for exactly this reason). The layout asserts now pass as-is;
also add the previously-missing static assert for builtin_entry_table_,
the one unchecked IsolateData field whose silent misplacement corrupts
builtin entry loads.

No behavior change on platforms with 1-byte bool.
